    description: "Split out of INV-pitab symptom 4 (2026-06-18 interview decision). DISTINCT in kind\nfrom INV-pitab's other symptoms: those were field-semantics bugs (a declared field\nlying about its meaning); this is a COVERAGE gap (whole categories of work have no\nprovenance card at all).\n\nProblem: ~half of a full `hypergumbo` run's wall-clock is non-pass overhead —\nframework discovery, file-index build, embedding-cache priming, gitleaks, repo\nfingerprinting, schema validation, JSON serialization — and none of it gets an\nAnalysisRun entry. Original repro (INV-pitab): total ~114s, pass span ~56s; the\nmissing ~58s is unaccounted at the provenance level, so \"where did the time go\" is\nunanswerable from analysis_runs[].\n\nDesign note: probably wants a NEW lightweight timing block\n(`overhead_phases: [{phase, duration_ms}]`), NOT reused AnalysisRun cards — those\ncarry pass-specific fields (pass_version, toolchain, nodes_emitted) meaningless for\n\"JSON serialization took 4s\". Schema addition + timers around ~6 phases.\n\nStatus: todo_soft / build deferred — lower value than correctness work; pick up when\noverhead observability is prioritized (candidate for a later wave)."  # 3e4f
    fields: {}  # 3e4f
- op: discuss  # 2852
  at: '2026-08-17T08:44:50Z'  # 2852
  by: agent  # 2852
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2852
  clock: 2  # 2852
  nonce: '2852'  # 2852
  message: "RE-PRICED WITH A NUMBER: non-pass overhead is 71% of a cold run on this repo,\nup from the ~51% that INV-hukap (deleted) recorded. And it is the single largest\nitem in the local edit-test cycle.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-17. One cold `hypergumbo slice --files` on hypergumbo itself,\n489 s wall, 96 passes. Per-pass timers from the cached survey:\n\n    ANALYZERS            106.2 s     21.7% of wall\n    LINKERS               36.6 s      7.5%\n    NON-PASS OVERHEAD    346.2 s     70.8%   <- invisible in analysis_runs\n\nSlowest passes, for scale: python 59.6 s (1,114 files), bash 40.3 s (43 files),\nsubprocess-linker 16.8 s. Everything else is under 5 s.\n\nSAME SPLIT ON TWO OTHER REPOS, so this is not a hypergumbo-only artifact:\n    whisper.cpp (565 src, 201 s wall)   passes 163.3 s   overhead  37.7 s (19%)\n    small py tree (67 files, 33 s wall) passes   7.0 s   overhead  26.0 s (79%)\nThe overhead does NOT scale with pass time -- it is large on a trivial repo and\nlarge on a big one, which is what makes it worth instrumenting rather than\nestimating.\n\nWHERE I BELIEVE IT GOES, stated as a hypothesis with the evidence that suggests\nit, NOT as a finding. The cache directory that this single `slice --files`\ninvocation wrote (mtimes 04:17-04:18, inside the 04:10:49-04:18:58 run):\n\n    survey.json         203 MB\n    survey.4k.json  |  survey.16k.json  |  survey.64k.json   (sketch fan-out)\n    survey.slices/      16 per-route handler slices\n    total               195 MB on disk\n\nSo a call whose question was \"which test files depend on these 2 source files\"\n-- answer: 9 files -- serialized a 203 MB behavior map, computed centrality,\nproduced three tiered previews and 16 handler slices. `survey` already has\n--no-sketch-fan-out and --no-handler-slices (from WI-rimos / WI-pijal, which\naddressed the same side-emission problem for `run`), which suggests the\nmachinery to skip this exists and the slice path simply does not use it.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN THE INCREMENTAL-ANALYSIS STREAM. WI-kazas/WI-zagij/\nWI-madut all attack the 142.8 s of passes. Even a perfect incremental analyzer\nthat re-analyzed only the 2 changed files leaves 346 s untouched, which is still\n~6 minutes per local test run. Seven consecutive smart-test runs this session\ntook 9m39s, 9m32s, 8m49s, 9m05s, 9m08s, 9m17s, 8m30s. See WI-runiz.\n\nFIRST STEP, cheap: instrument the non-pass phases (serialize / centrality /\nsketch fan-out / handler slices / cache write) and print the split, then check\nwhether `slice --files` can decline the side outputs it never reads. If the\nhypothesis above holds, that is a small change against 71% of the wall clock,\nand it is measurable BEFORE building either incremental-analysis design.\n\nP3 -> P1, todo_soft -> todo_hard. This is now the highest-value unmeasured thing\nin the perf area, and it gates whether WI-zagij is worth building at all."  # 2852
