- op: discuss  # 0657
  at: '2026-08-17T11:02:46Z'  # 0657
  by: agent  # 0657
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0657
  clock: 5  # 0657
  nonce: '0657'  # 0657
  message: "MEASURED AND FIXED. The hypothesis in the note above is REFUTED; the real cost was\nsomewhere else entirely, and is now positively attributed rather than inferred by\nsubtraction.\n\nINSTRUMENT. scripts/measure-survey-phase-split.py wraps ~36 phase functions of\nrun_survey with inclusive/exclusive accounting (nested calls are charged to the\nnearest wrapped ancestor, so only exclusive time is summed against the wall clock\nand the remainder prints as UNATTRIBUTED instead of inflating a neighbour). The\nphase MARKERS already existed — run_survey passes phase names to a nested\nshow_progress closure and throws the timing away — but a closure cannot be patched\nfrom outside, so the phase FUNCTIONS are wrapped instead.\n\nTWO PROBE DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED BEFORE READING ANY RESULT, both of the\ncheck-the-control kind:\n  - Two different functions are named run_all_analyzers (analyze/registry.py and\n    analyze/all_analyzers.py). Taking the first name match patched the one cli does\n    NOT call, so the analyzer phase read 0.0s while the pass cards it reconciles\n    against read 0.4s. Every homonym is now wrapped.\n  - analysis_runs is list[dict], not list[AnalysisRun], and to_dict renames pass_id\n    to `pass`. Reading the attribute made all 96 rows <unknown> at 0.0s. The script\n    now warns if cards exist with a zero duration sum rather than printing a silent\n    zero.\n\nRESULT, cold run_survey on hypergumbo itself, 517.7s wall, attribution reconciling\nto 95.7%:\n\n    graph/stamp_symbol_fingerprints    324.3s   62.6%   <-- one function\n    passes/run_all_analyzers            65.0s   12.6%\n    sketch/mention_centrality_batch     27.3s    5.3%\n    passes/run_all_linkers              28.9s    5.6%\n    sketch/_extract_config_info         15.4s    3.0%\n    graph/_classify_symbols             10.8s    2.1%\n    sideout/tier previews + slices       9.4s    1.8%\n    io/json dump (19 calls, 204 MB)      3.0s    0.6%\n    UNATTRIBUTED                        22.4s    4.3%\n\nTHE SIDE-OUTPUT HYPOTHESIS IS DEAD. A --lean counterfactual arm (no sketch fan-out,\nno handler slices, no sketch pre-computation) on the same tree: 517.7s -> 455.0s. The\nwhole side-output family is 62.7s, ~12% of wall, not 71%. Serializing the 203 MB\nsurvey.json is 3.0s. Both hypotheses were plausible; both were wrong.\n\nALSO CORRECTS THE EARLIER SPLIT. The 22%/7%/71% figures on this item were built by\nsumming per-pass duration_ms, which does not sum to wall clock: 96 cards total 157.7s\ninside a run_all_analyzers+run_all_linkers span of 93.9s wall. Card sums overstate\nwall share. Real wall shares: analyzers 12.6%, linkers 5.6%, fingerprint post-pass\n62.6%.\n\nMECHANISM, tested by prediction (scripts/measure-fingerprint-stamp-cost.py).\nstamp_symbol_fingerprints caches one parsed tree per (path, language) — so each file\nis PARSED once, as its docstring claims — but the per-symbol locator\n(_python_context_fingerprint) found the smallest AST node covering the span by\nwalking the WHOLE module again. The tree cache had removed repeated PARSING but not\nrepeated LOCATION, so cost was symbols(file) x nodes(file): quadratic in file size,\na big file expensive twice over. Evidence: ns/node measured FLAT at ~495 across the\nwhole file-size range (479.6-509.6 for ordinary files) — the signature of a per-call\nfull-tree scan, cost tracking the tree rather than the span. Feeding production's own\nsymbols-per-file distribution through that constant predicts 221s against 324s\nmeasured; the shortfall is ns/node rising to ~795 on the largest trees, which are\nexactly the top contributors (test_framework_patterns.py alone: 1,001 symbols x 81,233\nnodes). 41,584 of 42,575 stampable symbols are python, so the python branch is 97.7%\nof the population.\n\nFIX. The locator bisects a per-tree index bucketed by effective start line and scans\nstart lines DESCENDING, breaking once the best extent reachable at the current start\nline is worse than the incumbent (a covering node has n_end >= end_line, so extent\ncannot fall below end_line - n_start, and that bound only grows as n_start shrinks).\n\n    stamp_symbol_fingerprints   324.3s -> 22.8s   (14.2x)\n    cold run_survey wall        517.7s -> 220.0s  (2.35x, 297.7s saved)\n\nSEMANTIC PRESERVATION WAS THE REAL CONSTRAINT, since a fingerprint is serialized\noutput. The old rule was min extent with strict `<` against ast.walk's breadth-first\norder, which means ON AN EQUAL-EXTENT TIE THE OUTER NODE WINS (helper(1) on one line\nis covered by an Expr and by the Call inside it at identical extent). The index\ncarries each node's walk ordinal and takes the lexicographic min of\n(extent, ordinal), reproducing that exactly. The tempting rewrite — descend to the\ndeepest covering node — inverts the tie, hashes a different subtree and silently\nchanges fingerprints; test_fingerprint_locate_index.py pins the tie and ships a\npositive control proving the reference can distinguish the two rules, so\n\"production matches reference\" is not a vacuous claim.\n\nEQUIVALENCE VERIFIED AT REPO SCALE, not just in unit tests\n(scripts/compare-survey-fingerprints.py, before-survey vs after-survey):\n1 of 42,543 fingerprints changed, and it is the file-level fingerprint of\nfingerprint.py itself — the file edited between the two runs. Node-set deltas (21\nbefore-only, 53 after-only) are entirely inside the three files created or edited.\nSo zero drift among symbols not touched. Full suite: 5,718 passed, 100% coverage,\nmypy ratchet clean.\n\nHONEST LIMITS. 220.0s is a single trial, as was the 517.7s baseline (the two pre-fix\narms agreed within 1.8% on this phase, which is why the direction is trustworthy but\nthe second decimal is not). The tree-sitter branch of the same locator was NOT\nexamined for the same defect — it is only ~1,000 symbols on this repo, so the\nmeasurement here cannot see it; a repo dominated by a tree-sitter language would be\nthe discriminating test.\n"  # 0657
- op: discuss  # 7485
  at: '2026-08-17T11:04:23Z'  # 7485
  by: agent  # 7485
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7485
  clock: 6  # 7485
  nonce: '7485'  # 7485
  message: "SCOPE EXPANSION CHECKED — the tree-sitter branch does NOT share the defect, which\nretires the open limit stated in the note above (I said it had not been examined;\nit has been now).\n\n_tree_sitter_context_fingerprint locates via tree.root_node.descendant_for_byte_range\n— tree-sitter's native O(depth) descent — so it never scanned the whole tree. The\nasymmetry explains itself: the python branch had no equivalent stdlib API on\nast.Module, so the covering-node search was hand-written as a linear walk, and the\nhand-written one is the one that went quadratic. Same-construct-different-language\ncheck is therefore negative, and no sibling item is warranted.\n\nOne residual, recorded so it is not re-derived: the tree-sitter branch was only\n~1,000 of 42,575 stampable symbols on this repo, so this repo could not have\nmeasured it either way. The check above is by CODE READING of the locate strategy,\nnot by measurement."  # 7485
